'''Adephaga''' (a-def′a̤-gä̤), ''n. pl.'' [NL., neut. pl., < Gr. ἀδηφάγος: see ''adephagous.''] A group of voracious, carnivorous, and predatory beetles, composing a part of the pentamerous division of the order ''Coleoptera.'' {{smaller|They have filiform antennæ and but two palpi to each maxilla. Of the four families which make up this group, two, ''Gyrinidæ'' and ''Dytiscidæ'', are aquatic, and sometimes called ''Hydradephaga''; the other two, ''Carabidæ'' and ''Cicindelidæ'', are chiefly terrestrial, and are sometimes called ''Geodephaga.'' The whirligig and the tiger-beetle respectively exemplify these two divisions of ''Adephaga.'' Also called ''Carnivora.'' See cuts under ''Dytiscus'' and ''Cicindela.''}}

'''adephagan''' (a-def′a̤-gan), ''n.'' A beetle of the group ''Adephaga.''

'''adephagia''' (ad-ẹ̄-fā′ji-ä̤), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. ἀδηφαγία, < ἀδηφάγος, eating one’s fill, gluttonous: see ''adephagous.''] In ''pathol.'', voracious appetite; bulimia.

'''adephagous''' (a-def′a̤-gus), ''a.'' [< NL. ''adephagus'', < Gr. ἀδηφάγος, eating one’s fill, gluttonous, < ἀδην, or ἄδην, abundantly, enough (cf. L. ''satis'', enough), + φαγεῖν, eat.] Gluttonous; of or pertaining to the ''Adephaga'': as, ''adephagous'' beetles.

